courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-.text:
We prove that the positive-dimensional spheres admitting almost complex structures are exactly \(S^2\) and \(S^6\). The characteristic-class argument is the one discussed by Konstantis--Parton, Sections 3--6. We supply the needed generation step in complex K-theory by polynomial clutching, so its required integrality does not rest on an unproved appeal to Bott periodicity. The same finite linearization is developed in the CC0 supporting course \emph{K-theory for operator algebras}, lesson \emph{Bott periodicity}, \cref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:section-5}; its full Toeplitz proof gives periodicity for a larger class of coefficient algebras than we need here.

\subsubsection{Bundles, clutching, and based loops}
\label{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:section-10.1}

All bases in Sections 10.1--10.3 are compact Hausdorff spaces. For such a space \(Y\), \(K^0(Y)\) is the group completion of the direct-sum monoid of finite-rank complex vector bundles. Thus its elements are differences \([E]-[F]\), with the relation that adding the same bundle to both terms does not change the difference. Tensor product makes this a ring.

We use two elementary bundle facts, with their proofs. Every bundle \(E\) over compact \(Y\) embeds in a trivial bundle: choose finitely many trivializations and a partition of unity \(\rho_i\) subordinate to them, and send \(v\in E_y\) to the list \((\sqrt{\rho_i(y)}v_i)_i\) of its local coordinates, with zero entries off the corresponding supports. At least one \(\rho_i(y)\) is positive, so the map is injective on each fibre. Its image is a subbundle, and the orthogonal complement gives \(E\oplus E^\perp\simeq\mathbf1^N\). The orthogonal projection onto the image is continuous, as is seen in a local frame by the formula \(T(T^*T)^{-1}T^*\).

Second, bundles pulled back by homotopic maps from a compact space are isomorphic. Embed the bundle over the product with the homotopy interval in a trivial bundle as just described, and let \(p(y,t)\) be its range projection. Uniform continuity gives a finite partition of the interval on which consecutive projections differ in norm by less than one, uniformly in \(y\). For projections \(p,q\) with \(\|p-q\|<1\), the map \(q:\operatorname{im}p\to\operatorname{im}q\) is injective, since \(\|qv\|\geq(1-\|p-q\|)\|v\|\). Reversing \(p,q\) gives equal ranks, so it is an isomorphism; its inverse is continuous in local finite matrices. Composing these finite isomorphisms proves the assertion. This proof applies equally with a compact disk included in the base.

Apply these facts to a bundle on \(S^2\times Y\). Its restrictions to the closed hemispheres are pullbacks of bundles on \(Y\), by contraction of the disks. Their restrictions at one equatorial point identify those two bundles, say with \(E_0\). Adding the pullback of a complement of \(E_0\) makes both hemisphere bundles trivial. The stabilized bundle is then obtained from a clutching map

\begin{equation}
g:Y\times\mathbb T\longrightarrow GL_r(\mathbb C).
\label{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:eq:10.1}
\end{equation}

courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-th.text:
\subsection{The complete complement map for a framed handle}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:belt-complement-map}

\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-1}\label{courseIII:belt-complement:1}

Let \(N\) be a closed smooth manifold of dimension \(d-1\). Fix an integer \(1\leq k\leq d-1\), put \(q=d-k\), and retain positive radii \(a,b\). Write \(D_a^k=\{x\in\mathbb R^k:\|x\|\leq a\}\) and \(S_a^{k-1}=\partial D_a^k\), and use the analogous notation with \(q,b\). The specified framing is a smooth embedding

\begin{equation}
\varphi:S_a^{k-1}\times D_b^q\longrightarrow N.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.1}
\end{equation}

Its attaching core and closed exterior are

\begin{equation}
L=\varphi(S_a^{k-1}\times\{0\}),\qquad
A=N\setminus\varphi(S_a^{k-1}\times\operatorname{int}D_b^q).
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:1.2}

\subsection{Avoiding the cores by an actual ambient isotopy}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:core-avoidance}

\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-2}\label{courseIII:belt-complement:2}

We next prove what deleting the cores does to homotopy groups. This proof is for the given framed tubes, so it neither assumes a global transversality theorem nor suppresses the normal-coordinate data.

Let \(P\) be a compact smooth manifold, possibly with boundary, of dimension \(r<q\). Let \(u:P\to N\) be smooth and suppose its values on a closed prescribed set \(Q\subset P\) avoid the cores. We will find an ambient isotopy \(T_t:N\to N\) such that \(T_tu=u\) on \(Q\) for all \(t\), and \(T_1u(P)\) misses all the cores.

For one tube, the compact set \(u(Q)\) is disjoint from \(L\). Choose a radius \(0<\delta<b\) so that

\begin{equation}
u(Q)\cap\varphi(S_a^{k-1}\times D_\delta^q)=\varnothing.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:2.1}
\end{equation}

To see that such a choice exists, otherwise a sequence of points of the compact \(u(Q)\) would have normal radii tending to zero in the compact closed tube, and a subsequence would limit to a point of \(u(Q)\cap L\). This is a contradiction. If \(Q\) is empty, choose any \(\delta\) in the displayed interval.

\subsection{The exact homotopy comparison and the middle level}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:belt-homotopy}

\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-3}\label{courseIII:belt-complement:3}

Assume \(q=d-k\geq2\), and choose a basepoint in the common exterior \(A\). Let \(U=N\setminus\bigcup_jL_j\). The inclusion

\begin{equation}
\iota_U:U\hookrightarrow N
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.1}
\end{equation}

is a bijection on components. Surjectivity follows by moving any point off the cores with the dimension-zero case of Section 2. If two points in \(U\) are connected in \(N\), smooth a connecting path relative its two endpoints and apply the avoidance argument with \(r=1<q\). Its final path lies in \(U\) with exactly the same endpoints. This proves injectivity on components.

For each \(1\leq i\leq q-1\), a based \(\pi_i(N)\) representative is a disk map with its whole boundary at the basepoint. Smooth it relative a constant boundary collar as just proved. Its domain has dimension \(i<q\); avoidance fixes its boundary and gives a representative in \(U\) of the same class in \(N\). Thus

\begin{equation}
(\iota_U)_*:\pi_i(U)\longrightarrow\pi_i(N)

\subsection{Embedded disks with the prescribed collar fixed}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:relative-embedded-disks}

\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-5}\label{courseIII:belt-complement:5}

We prove the disk construction in a five-dimensional smooth target by a finite parameter family. The construction keeps a given boundary collar pointwise fixed and retains an already avoided closed set. It also removes intersections with specified two-dimensional submanifolds. The different dimensions of the attaching and belt spheres will therefore enter through different, proved maps.

Let \(M\) be a closed smooth five-dimensional manifold. Retain a disk of radius \(h>0\),

\begin{equation}
P=D_h^2=\{p=(p_1,p_2):p_1^2+p_2^2\leq h^2\}.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:5.1}
\end{equation}

Choose \(0<r_1<r_0<h\), and let \(u:P\to M\) be smooth, with an embedded restriction to the collar \(\{r_1<\|p\|\leq h\}\). The fixed collar will be

\begin{equation}
C_0=\{r_0\leq\|p\|\leq h\}.

\subsection{The exact obstruction to extending a rank-three normal frame}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:rank-three-framing}

\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-6}\label{courseIII:belt-complement:6}

The disk just constructed has three normal directions in the five-dimensional target. We now compute its frame-extension obstruction and an operation that changes it while preserving a specified first normal vector on a chosen boundary arc. This is a proof about the actual normal bundle of an embedded disk. \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-7} constructs the Whitney boundary with its required sheet data and applies this criterion while retaining its full nonorthogonal frames.

\subsubsection{A normal frame over the full original disk}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-6.1}

Let \(F:D_h^2\to M^5\) be a smooth embedded disk, with the metric induced by the fixed embedding \(\iota:M\to\mathbb R^m\). Its normal bundle inside \(M\) is the rank-three subbundle

\begin{equation}
E_p=\{v\in T_{F(p)}M:
 \langle v,DF_p(w)\rangle=0\text{ for every }w\in\mathbb R^2\}.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:6.1}
\end{equation}

The disk just constructed has three normal directions in the five-dimensional target. We now compute its frame-extension obstruction and an operation that changes it while preserving a specified first normal vector on a chosen boundary arc. This is a proof about the actual normal bundle of an embedded disk. \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-7} constructs the Whitney boundary with its required sheet data and applies this criterion while retaining its full nonorthogonal frames.

\subsubsection{A normal frame over the full original disk}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-6.1}

Let \(F:D_h^2\to M^5\) be a smooth embedded disk, with the metric induced by the fixed embedding \(\iota:M\to\mathbb R^m\). Its normal bundle inside \(M\) is the rank-three subbundle

\begin{equation}
E_p=\{v\in T_{F(p)}M:
 \langle v,DF_p(w)\rangle=0\text{ for every }w\in\mathbb R^2\}.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:6.1}
\end{equation}

Here and below \(DF\) is viewed in the original ambient coordinates through \(D\iota\). Write \(P_M(p)\) for the orthogonal projection onto \(T_{F(p)}M\), and write \(P_D(p)\) for the projection onto \(DF_p(\mathbb R^2)\). The latter is explicit: if \(A(p)\) is the \(m\)-by-\(2\) matrix with columns \(D(\iota F)_p(\partial_1)\), \(D(\iota F)_p(\partial_2)\), then

\begin{equation}
P_D=A(A^{\mathsf t}A)^{-1}A^{\mathsf t},
\qquad P=P_M-P_D.

\subsubsection{Keeping the original nonorthogonal frame}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:full-original-frame}

\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-6.6}

The sheet directions in a Whitney construction need not be perpendicular in the original metric. We therefore extend the criterion without replacing those directions. Let \(e\) now be any smooth positive ordered frame of \(E\), and retain its full matrix \(R\) in the orthonormal reference frame \(n\) from \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:6.5}. Thus \(e=nR\), with \(R\in GL^+(3,\mathbb R)\). For its three columns \(r_1,r_2,r_3\), put

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
t_{11}&=\|r_1\|,&q_1&=r_1/t_{11},\\
t_{12}&=\langle q_1,r_2\rangle,&
w_2&=r_2-t_{12}q_1,\\
t_{22}&=\|w_2\|,&q_2&=w_2/t_{22},\\
t_{13}&=\langle q_1,r_3\rangle,&
t_{23}&=\langle q_2,r_3\rangle,\\
w_3&=r_3-t_{13}q_1-t_{23}q_2,\\
t_{33}&=\|w_3\|,&q_3&=w_3/t_{33}.
\end{aligned}

\subsection{The original Whitney boundary and its framed embedded disk}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:whitney-boundary}

\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-7}\label{courseIII:belt-complement:7}

We now construct the geometric boundary needed by Sections 5--6. This statement concerns a specified handle level; its hypotheses describe exactly the level to which it applies. Let \(N'\) be obtained from a connected, oriented, simply connected closed five-manifold \(N\) by the finite disjoint framed index-two surgeries of \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-1}, with the original attaching maps and radii retained. Let \(B_1,\ldots,B_r\) be their belt three-spheres. Let \(A_1,\ldots,A_s\) be disjoint oriented embedded two-spheres, each transverse to every \(B_j\). Choose two opposite-sign intersections \(p,q\) of one \(A=A_i\) with one \(B=B_j\).

We will construct an embedded disk with two boundary corners, whose boundary consists of an arc in \(A\) and an arc in \(B\), whose interior avoids all the \(A_\ell,B_k\), and whose full normal frame has the required one-direction/two-direction boundary data. The later handle argument must put the original cobordism into this specified stage. This disk theorem does not assume that reduction has already been carried out for the course's cobordism.

\subsubsection{Arcs and transverse endpoint charts}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-7.1}

The transverse intersections form finite sets. Transversality makes each intersection locally isolated, and compactness then makes the closed discrete intersection set finite. Choose smooth embedded arcs
\(\alpha,\beta:[0,1]\to N'\), both from \(p\) to \(q\), with
\(\alpha\subset A\), \(\beta\subset B\), and with their interiors avoiding every intersection point with the other sphere system. Such arcs can be chosen inside either sphere with the finitely many forbidden points deleted. To justify this without an arc-existence assumption, first join the endpoints by finitely many coordinate segments in the connected sphere; detour around each forbidden point in a small coordinate ball of dimension at least two. Choose the finitely many segment vertices so overlapping pieces are not identical and the resulting crossings are isolated. In dimension two a local perturbation of straight segments gives finitely many crossings; in dimension three it can also separate them. Erase successive loops at crossings to obtain a simple piecewise smooth arc, and round its finitely many corners in disjoint small coordinate balls. Keep prescribed short endpoint arcs unchanged throughout. This gives the required embedded smooth arcs. Their interiors cannot meet each other, since any such meeting would be an avoided point of \(A\cap B\).

At \(p\) choose a coordinate map \(\psi\) on the original \(N'\), together with parametrizations
\(a_p(x,u)\) of \(A\) and \(b_p(y,v,w)\) of \(B\), taking zero to \(p\), so their first coordinate axes give the chosen endpoint arcs with their specified parameters. Define

Their interiors, where \(x,y>0\), avoid both sheets by \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:7.2} and the injectivity of the charts. Shrink the two disjoint endpoint neighbourhoods so they avoid every other sphere.

\subsubsection{Strips joining the two quadrants}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-7.2}

Along the interior of \(\alpha\), choose a smooth vector field \(\eta_A\) transverse to \(TA\). Near \(p\) it must equal the \(y\)-coordinate field of \(\mathcal H_p\), and near \(q\) the \(y\)-coordinate field of \(\mathcal H_q\). These prescribed endpoint fields are transverse. The choices between them exist because the quotient \(TN'|_\alpha/TA|_\alpha\) is a rank-three bundle over an interval. A frame of that bundle is obtained by the projector transport \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:6.3} along the interval. In that frame the two endpoint nonzero vectors can be joined through \(\mathbb R^3\setminus\{0\}\), for example by two straight segments through a third vector not on either forbidden ray, followed by smoothing with the endpoints fixed. Interpolate the remaining tangent components independently. Taking the interpolation constant near each prescribed endpoint neighbourhood preserves every already specified derivative.

Along \(\beta\), choose \(\eta_B\) transverse to \(TB\), equal to the \(x\)-coordinate fields in the endpoint charts. The same argument now uses the connected space \(\mathbb R^2\setminus\{0\}\). A path through it can be written by a continuous angle and positive radius along the interval; interpolation of the angle and positive radius, constant near the prescribed endpoint pieces, gives the required smooth path with no zero.

Extend each vector field to an ambient neighbourhood, using local coordinate extensions and a partition of unity equal to the original field along the arc. Make the extensions exactly the respective coordinate fields near the endpoints. Their local flows define the two strips

\begin{equation}
S_A(s,t)=\operatorname{Fl}^{\,t}_{\eta_A}(\alpha(s)),\qquad
S_B(s,t)=\operatorname{Fl}^{\,t}_{\eta_B}(\beta(s)),\qquad t\geq0.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:7.4}
\end{equation}

Near each endpoint these formulas agree exactly with \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:7.3}, by uniqueness of the flow of the coordinate field. At \(t=0\), each strip derivative has rank two. Compactness gives a uniform positive width on which they are embeddings: a sequence of new double points with widths tending to zero would limit either to two distinct points of the embedded original arc, or to the same point where a local embedding chart excludes them. The same argument keeps the strips apart away from their prescribed overlapping endpoint quadrants.

courseIII:creating-an-original-cancellin.text:
\subsection{The lower attaching sphere bounds its framed disk}
\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:pair-attaching-disk}

\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-6}\label{courseIII:pair-creation:6}

Write \(h_m=h(u_m)\). The lower unstable disk down to the original model \(a\)-level is exactly

\begin{equation}
D_q=\{(u_m,y,0):A(y)\leq h_m-a\}.
 \label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:6.1}
\end{equation}

It is embedded, its boundary lies in \(F_b^{-1}(a)\), and its interior is the lower unstable manifold truncated at that level. Apply \(H^{-1}\) to its boundary. Since \(H(q_0,a)=j(q_0)\), \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:2.6} gives the exact lower attaching sphere in the original height product:

\begin{equation}
t=a,\quad z=0,\quad A(y_0)=u_m-a,\qquad
 y_0=\sqrt{\frac{u_m-a}{h_m-a}}\,y.
 \label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:6.2}

courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index.text:
\subsection{The old two-handles and their exact descending comparison}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:slide-original-flow}

\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-6}\label{courseIII:low-index-slides:6}

The following preparation makes the use of the complement map precise. Start with the actual ordered Morse function and field supplied by \cref{courseIII:morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering:trajectory-ordered-morse}, after zero/top removal. If there is no one-handle, no index-one cancellation is needed. Otherwise select the currently highest index-one critical point \(q\). Write its value as \(c_1\). Keep the full local coordinates and Hessian coefficients of this point.

\subsubsection{Putting the old two-handles at one critical value}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-6.1}

There must be at least one old two-handle: if there were none, \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:1.9} would identify the nontrivial free group of the remaining one-handles with the trivial group of \(C\). Let \(c_2\) initially be the lowest old index-two critical value. We change the higher index-two critical values to this same \(c_2\), without changing any critical point, its index or its local Hessian.

Here is a finite induction which realizes the change. Suppose the first \(j\) old index-two points already have value \(c_2\), and take the next old index-two point \(p_j\), at value \(c_j>c_2\). Choose a regular level \(a\) with

\begin{equation}
c_1<a<c_2.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:6.1}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Removing the selected index-one handle}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:slide-index-one-cancellation}

\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-7}\label{courseIII:low-index-slides:7}

We now apply all these constructions on the original cobordism. Keep the simultaneous old two-handle stage of Section 6. In a regular band above \(b\) and below the first old higher-index critical value, or below the outgoing collar if there is no such point, insert the supported two/three pair. Use a small coordinate disk in \(H(e(E))\) for its projection to the old level. The full compact support and the entire lower attaching disk of the pair fit in that coordinate product by the explicit positive scales of \cref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section}, Section 5. Its \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-6} computes the disk and its full extended frame. Denote the new index-two point by \(p\) and its paired index-three point by \(r\). Their values satisfy

\begin{equation}
c_2<b<f(p)<f(r).
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:7.1}
\end{equation}

Use a slightly smaller regular band below \(p\) to record the complete attaching tube pulled down to \(f=b\), and then through \(H^{-1}\). Call it

\begin{equation}
\alpha_0:S^1_{a_0}\times D^{n-2}_{b_0}\longrightarrow S.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:7.2}
\end{equation}

This is the reduction now proved. The \cref{courseIII:integral-handle-chains-and-signed-incidences:handle-six-seven} now proves the original filtration-to-homology map, every incidence sign, the full integer basis comparisons and the exact contractions in dimensions six and seven. The \cref{courseIII:higher-index-framed-slides-and-chain-maps:higher-slide-matrices} now realizes every elementary integral change with its full framed cobordism map and proves both neighbouring differentials using the singular-prism correction. The \cref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:receiving-whitney-flow} proves the full disk, adapted frame and supported move for the three required sheet-dimension pairs, with all original metric, orientation and framing maps retained. The \cref{courseIII:realizing-framed-handles-and-middle-cancellation:middle-six-seven-reduction} constructs the compatible function, proves the exact intersection-to-trajectory comparison and every escape and support hypothesis, and completes the simply connected smooth cobordism reductions in dimensions six and seven.

\subsubsection{A proved strengthening of the incoming hypothesis}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-8.1}

The forward removal actually uses a weaker hypothesis than simple connectivity. Here is the exact statement and its proof. Let \(M_-\) be connected and suppose that its actual inclusion induces a surjection

\begin{equation}
\iota_*:\pi_1(M_-,b)\longrightarrow\pi_1(C,b).
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:8.6}
\end{equation}

After zero/top removal, all index-one handles can still be removed by the construction above.

Put \(\Gamma=\pi_1(M_-,b)\), keeping its full multiplication and all its relations. Removing the finitely many attaching disks induces an isomorphism on this group: surjectivity moves loops off their centres and radially out; injectivity does the same to null-homotopies relative their boundary, exactly as in \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:1.4}. The two-component path-class argument in \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-1} therefore gives

\begin{equation}
\pi_1(W_1,b)=\Gamma*F(x_1,\ldots,x_r),\qquad

courseIII:framed-manifolds-and-the-pontr.text:
\begin{equation}
S_m(v,a,b)=(Qa,\ P_\nu a+Jv,\ b),\qquad
 S_m^2=I,\qquad
 \tau_E=\tau_{\mathrm{st}}S_m.
 \label{courseIII:framed-manifolds-and-the-pontryagin-thom-map:eq:8.11}
\end{equation}

Each equality follows by substituting these three matrix identities. Relative to the decomposition \(a=P_\nu a+JQa\), the map \(S_m\) exchanges the two \(n\)-dimensional tangent summands and fixes every other summand. Its determinant sign is therefore \((-1)^n\). In general an orientation calculation alone would not make this bundle automorphism homotopic to the identity.

For the original \(M=X\), retain its original almost-complex tangent operator \(\mathcal J\), with \(\mathcal J^2=-I\). It is provided by the original complex charts, independently of the later smooth-sphere recognition. This gives the following explicit homotopy, which proves the additional comparison we actually need. For \(0\leq t\leq1\), put \(\theta=\pi t/2\), \(\varphi=\pi t\), \(u=Qa\), \(y=P_\nu a\), and set

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 C_\varphi&=\cos\varphi\,I+\sin\varphi\,\mathcal J,\qquad
       C_\varphi^{-1}=\cos\varphi\,I-\sin\varphi\,\mathcal J,\\
 v_t&=\cos\theta\,v+\sin\theta\,u,\\
 a_t&=y+J C_\varphi(-\sin\theta\,v+\cos\theta\,u),\\
 S_{m,t}(v,a,b)&=(v_t,a_t,b).
 \end{aligned}

